    rationale:
      "With NEXT_STEPS fully gated, dispatched three parallel research agents, each covering one AGENT_PROTOCOL empty-backlog priority tier (1: sold-capability wiring gap; 2: Convex/SQLite parity/idempotency; 3/4 combined: security/injection audit + untested orchestration), each explicitly told to verify any candidate against actual source rather than trusting this log's own clean-audit claims. All three returned concrete, independently-verifiable findings; picked the tier-2 finding to implement, since — unlike a missing visibility metric — it was a live correctness bug with direct real-world consequence (a genuine duplicate transactional email to an actual paying customer or prospect), and it directly contradicted an invariant AGENT_PROTOCOL itself already documents (\"Where a durable mark must precede a notification..., persist-then-notify: write the mark first, skip the send entirely on a ConvexPrimaryWriteError\") as well as its own correctly-implemented sibling (recordCheckoutRecoverySent, called before sendCheckoutAbandonedRecovery in sendCheckoutRecoveryAction).\n\nsendStarterUpsellAction (shipped in starter-upsell-send-race-guard, PR #869) and sendStripeConnectOnboardingLinkToClientAction (shipped in stripe-connect-onboarding-link-send-race-guard, PR #871, which that session's own commit explicitly says 'mirrors starter-upsell-send-race-guard') both called their real send function FIRST, then recorded the CAS (compare-and-swap) sent-marker afterward, with a comment explicitly reasoning through what happens when the marker write races — but never noticing that ordering choice defeats the guard's own stated purpose. A CAS write can only ever block ONE side of a race: with mark-after-send, two concurrent requests (a second open dashboard tab, or a double-click before the button's isPending-disabled state applies) both read the same starting sentCount, both pass eligibility, and BOTH calls send the real email before either one reaches the mark — the guard's false return only detected the collision after the fact, correctly reporting success: false for the losing call, but the real duplicate email had already gone out to the client. Independently reproduced by reading the exact code and tracing the async ordering: sendStarterUpsellEmail(...) at (pre-fix) line 2437, recordStarterUpsellSent(...) at line 2477 — a full ~40 lines and one full async round-trip apart, with nothing gating the send on the mark.\n\nFixed both actions by moving the CAS mark to run immediately before the send (mirroring recordCheckoutRecoverySent's call site in sendCheckoutRecoveryAction) and reusing the same ConvexPrimaryWriteError-specific catch pattern: `if (markError instanceof ConvexPrimaryWriteError) return { success: false, error: \"...email was not sent...\" }; throw markError;` — a fail-closed default that previously silently swallowed ANY error type (not just the expected ConvexPrimaryWriteError) and proceeded to send anyway (`let marked = true; try { marked = await recordX(...) } catch { /* log only */ }`), which was itself a second, smaller correctness gap this fix also closes. A lost CAS (marked === false) now returns before the send ever fires ('skipped duplicate send', not 'this click may have sent a duplicate email'). A genuine send failure AFTER a successful mark (network blip, suppression list, Resend API error) now surfaces with an explicit '(sent-marker was recorded — click ... again to retry)' note, since the marker's whole design (documented in starter-upsell-persist.ts / stripe-connect-onboarding-link-persist.ts) is a concurrency guard only, never a one-shot gate — a deliberate resend against a fresh read always succeeds.\n\nShipped: src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts — sendStarterUpsellAction and sendStripeConnectOnboardingLinkToClientAction both reordered mark-before-send, with updated error-message wording on every failure branch. src/app/dashboard/leads/send-starter-upsell-action.test.ts — the existing race-guard test rewritten to assert the send is never called on a lost CAS (was: called once, since it fired before the guard could catch it); the existing 'best-effort on a thrown write error' test replaced with two: a ConvexPrimaryWriteError-specific not-sent case (new coverage — the module now needs to mock @/lib/payments/convex-primary for identical class-reference instanceof checks across vi.resetModules(), mirroring checkout-recovery-actions.test.ts's own precedent) and a generic-error-fails-closed case (behavior change: previously succeeded silently after sending anyway; now correctly fails without sending); suppression/send-failure message assertions updated for the new retry-note suffix. src/app/dashboard/leads/stripe-connect-actions.test.ts — the mirror set of changes for the onboarding-link action (this file already mocked ConvexPrimaryWriteError at module scope, so no new mock was needed there), plus one new send-failure-after-mark test proving the mark is now recorded even when delivery fails. src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx — new paragraph before 'Tech stack' naming the gap, why the CAS guard couldn't actually prevent the race in mark-after-send order, and the fix. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — this is a real, previously-unaddressed correctness bug fix in two already-shipped capabilities' own duplicate-send guards, not any of the five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable), which remain operator/legal/infra-blocked and unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 3566a608 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#888).\n\nVerified: npm ci (413 packages, 9 known high-severity findings, unchanged dev-only) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (send-starter-upsell-action.test.ts + stripe-connect-actions.test.ts, 41/41 passed) then full suite (3513 passed, 210 files — 2 net new) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). File-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: business-decisions.ts 124,751 bytes, prompt_process.tsx 232,172 bytes at session start (both via wc -c) — business-decisions.ts comfortably under the 256KB trigger; prompt_process.tsx has real but shrinking headroom and should get a fresh wc -c check (and a re-archive, as its own dedicated iteration, if it has crossed 256KB) at the very start of the next session.",
